// GAP-ZYB-008 — an aggregate juxtaposes into a string, exactly as `>>` prints it.
//
// The tree-walker kept a whitelist for juxtaposition and `>>` did not use it,
// so the same juxtaposition of the same value gave two answers depending on
// where it was written:
//
//     >> "arr: " a ¶      // arr: [1, 2, 3]
//     s = "" a            // Runtime error: cannot juxtapose value of type Array
//
// An array could be printed, once, and never kept: not in a log line, not in an
// error message, not in a value to store, not in a test comparison. ZyBank's
// `verificación_dinero.zy` wanted to check the shape of a split by comparing
// `"" d::repartir(10000, 3)` against `"[3334, 3333, 3333]"` and had to compare
// element by element instead.
//
// The register VM and the browser engine never had the split. This is the
// tree-walker catching up to them, and to its own `>>`.
a = [1, 2, 3]
t = #(x: 1, y: "dos")
p = (10, 20)

s = "" a
>> "arreglo:     " s ¶
>> "mismo que >> " a ¶

>> "diccionario: " ("" t) ¶
>> "tupla:       " ("" p) ¶

// Inside a longer string, which is the point: it can be carried.
msg = "el reparto fue " a " y sobra 0"
>> msg ¶

// Nested, and through a function.
m = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
describir(v) { <~ "<" v ">" }
>> describir(m) ¶

// And it compares as text, which is what a suite wants.
>> (("" a) == "[1, 2, 3]") ¶
